    "pros": [
      "About 24-25 g protein per 28 g scoop, per the label",
      "Low lactose and almost no carbohydrate",
      "Neutral taste mixes into coffee and cold drinks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more per 25 g than the concentrates here",
      "Unflavored only"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The budget answer for anyone who needs isolate for lactose or macros.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 24-25 g",
      "Calories": "About 110",
      "Third-party tested": "Informed Sport (confirm)",
      "Best for": "Isolate on a budget"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "now-sports-pea-protein",
    "name": "NOW Sports Pea Protein (Unflavored)",
    "brand": "NOW Foods",
    "badge": "Best Budget Plant",
    "category": "Pea protein isolate (unflavored)",
    "description": "NOW's unflavored pea protein delivers about 24 g of protein per scoop for roughly 120 calories, per the label, at one of the lowest costs per 25 g of protein in the plant category, especially in the larger bags. NOW runs its own in-house testing laboratory, which matters more for plant protein than for dairy given the heavy metal concerns raised by recent independent testing. Like every unflavored pea isolate, it tastes earthy on its own and works best blended.",
    "pros": [
      "About 24 g protein per scoop, per the label",
      "One of the cheapest plant proteins per 25 g",
      "Brand runs in-house testing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Earthy taste and gritty texture in water",
      "Slightly less leucine per gram than whey"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest credible plant protein for smoothies, oats and baking.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 24 g",
      "Calories": "About 120",
      "Third-party tested": "Brand in-house lab testing",
      "Best for": "Vegan and dairy-free budgets"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cheap Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-cheap-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the cheapest way to buy protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy a plain whey concentrate or a single-ingredient pea protein in the largest size you will finish, ideally on sale, and compare products on cost per 25 g of protein rather than tub price. Unflavored versions usually give the most protein per scoop for the money, and you can flavor them cheaply at home."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is cheap protein powder bad for you?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not inherently. Many budget powders are simple whey or pea protein with a short ingredient list. The risks at the low end are inflated protein claims and, for plant products, heavy metals. Choose products with disclosed formulas and some form of independent testing, and a cheap powder can be just as good as an expensive one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I calculate cost per gram of protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Divide the price by the number of servings to get cost per serving, then divide that by the grams of protein per serving to get cost per gram. Multiply by 25 for cost per 25 g. Using the same unit for every product cancels out differences in tub size and scoop size."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey concentrate as good as isolate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For building muscle, yes, when the protein dose is matched. Concentrate contains a little more lactose, fat and carbohydrate per scoop, which matters if you are lactose intolerant or counting calories very closely. For most people it delivers nearly the same result as isolate for noticeably less money."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is amino spiking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Amino spiking is adding cheap nitrogen-containing ingredients such as glycine, taurine or creatine to a protein product so that a nitrogen-based lab test reports more protein than is really there. It was the subject of a wave of US class-action lawsuits starting in 2014. Reading the ingredients list is the best defense."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are budget plant protein powders safe?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many are, but plant proteins on average carry more heavy metals than dairy proteins, according to Consumer Reports testing in 2025. Look for published lab results or a recognized certification, and favor brands that answer questions about lead. Pregnant people and anyone with health conditions should ask a doctor or dietitian."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is protein powder cheaper than food?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes, not always. Milk, eggs, Greek yogurt, canned fish, lentils and chicken thighs are often competitive per 25 g of protein, depending on prices where you live. Powder usually wins on convenience and on protein per calorie, so it is most useful when you are short on time or dieting."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder",
    tags: [
      "best cheap protein powder",
      "budget friendly protein powder",
      "affordable protein powder",
      "cheap protein powder",
      "cost per gram of protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Budget Picks
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Cheap <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Protein Powder</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six budget-friendly powders ranked on what you actually pay for 25 g of real protein, and the label tricks that make some cheap tubs more expensive than they look.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#optimum-nutrition-gold-standard-100-whey"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#cost-math"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Do the Cost Math
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">{author.name}</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">{author.role}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-cheap-means" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Cheap Should Mean</a>
          <a href="#how-we-ranked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Ranked Them</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#cost-math" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Cost per 25 g Math</a>
          <a href="#false-economy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. When Cheap Is a False Economy</a>
          <a href="#label-tricks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Underdosed Blends and Scoop Tricks</a>
          <a href="#concentrate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Why Concentrate Wins on Budget</a>
          <a href="#plant-budget" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Cheap Plant Protein</a>
          <a href="#save-more" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Ways to Cut Cost Further</a>
          <a href="#pay-more" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. When Paying More Is Worth It</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The cheapest tub on the shelf is rarely the cheapest protein. What matters is how much you pay for each 25 g of genuine, honestly labelled protein, and on that measure a handful of plain, well-made powders beat almost everything else. This guide ranks six of them, shows you how to run the numbers on any tub in under a minute, and explains when a low price is hiding a protein you are not really getting.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-cheap-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Cheap Should Mean: Cost per 25 g, Not Cost per Tub
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shelf prices are designed to be compared badly. One tub is two pounds, the next is five, a third is sold by servings, and the serving sizes themselves range from a 25 g scoop to a 75 g double scoop. The sticker tells you what leaves your bank account today. It does not tell you what a gram of protein costs, which is the only thing you are buying a protein powder for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to compare every product on the same unit: the cost of 25 g of protein. We use 25 g because it is close to a typical single scoop and sits in the range that research on muscle protein synthesis suggests is a sensible per-meal dose for most adults. Convert every tub to that unit and the market sorts itself very quickly. A tub that looks expensive can turn out to be one of the cheapest per gram, and a bargain bin tub with a small protein dose per scoop can be one of the dearest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g is only half the test, though, because it assumes the label is telling the truth. A powder that claims 25 g of protein but delivers 19 g of real protein plus a few grams of cheap free amino acids is not cheap at all; you are paying for nitrogen that a lab test counts as protein but your muscles do not use the same way. So the second half of the test is honesty: a disclosed formula, no proprietary blends, and ideally some form of independent testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put those two together and the definition of a good cheap protein powder becomes simple. It is the lowest cost per 25 g of protein you can find among products whose protein figure you have a good reason to believe. Everything in this guide follows from that sentence, and once you can apply it yourself you will not need a ranking at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Ranked the Budget Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run our own lab tests, taste panels or price-tracking software for this guide. The ranking is built on label analysis, published certification status, the brands' own testing disclosures and the published research on protein dosing, the same method set out on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste or mixing, we are reporting what buyers widely say rather than claiming a result of our own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was cost. Every product here sits toward the lower end of the market on cost per 25 g of protein at typical retail prices we saw at the time of writing, especially in the larger tub or bag sizes. We did not chase the absolute lowest number on any given day, because sale prices rotate and a product that is cheapest this week is often mid-pack the next.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second filter was label honesty. We excluded anything with a proprietary protein blend that hides how much comes from each source, and anything listing free amino acids such as glycine or taurine alongside the protein in a way that could inflate the protein figure. We gave extra credit to products with an independent certification such as Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport, and some credit to brands that run documented in-house or third-party testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third filter was the protein dose itself. We favoured products that deliver close to 25 g of protein per serving, or that make it easy to get there, and we looked at protein as a share of the scoop weight, because a high share means you are paying for protein rather than flavouring, sweetener and filler. Finally, every pick had to be widely available and long established, with buyer feedback suggesting people actually finish the tub. If none of these suits you, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">whey protein value rankings</a> cover a wider price range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Cheap Protein Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each pick below clears the same bars: a disclosed formula, protein that comes from real protein rather than added amino acids, a long track record and a cost per 25 g of protein toward the low end of the market at typical retail prices. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Prices move constantly, so we describe cost in relative terms rather than quoting numbers that will be wrong next week.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
                    )}
                    <div>
                      <p class="text-white font-black text-lg">{product.name}</p>
                      <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#38BDF8]/20 text-[#38BDF8] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold hidden sm:block">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
