        <div id="cost-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Work Out Cost per 25 g in Under a Minute
          </h2>

          <p>
            You need three numbers from the tub: the price, the number of servings, and the grams of protein per serving. Divide the price by the number of servings to get cost per serving. Then divide 25 by the protein per serving and multiply the two. That gives you the cost of 25 g of protein, and you can compare any two products on it regardless of tub size or scoop size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the arithmetic with round, made-up numbers so you can see it work. A tub priced at 60 dollars with 60 servings costs 1 dollar per serving. If each serving has 20 g of protein, you need 1.25 servings to reach 25 g, so the cost per 25 g is 1.25 dollars. A second tub priced at 75 dollars with 60 servings of 30 g each costs 1.25 dollars per serving but only needs 0.83 servings to reach 25 g, so its cost per 25 g is about 1.04 dollars. The tub with the higher sticker price is the cheaper protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful cross-check is protein as a share of the scoop weight. Divide the grams of protein by the serving weight in grams. Plain isolates and unflavored single-ingredient powders often land between 85 and 90 percent. Flavored concentrates usually sit between about 70 and 80 percent. Anything well below 70 percent means a large part of each scoop is something other than protein, which is fine in a deliberate mass gainer and a warning sign in a product sold as a protein powder.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pick</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Serving weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein by weight</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g (2 scoops)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 83%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Sports Whey Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24-25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 28 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 86-89%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Myprotein Impact Whey (US)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20-21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 80-84%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">ON Gold Standard 100% Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30-31 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 77-80%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Whey Concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 36 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 69%</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Notice that the cheapest product per tub is not automatically the leanest per scoop. A flavored concentrate with a 69 percent protein share can still be an excellent buy if its price is low enough; the percentage simply tells you how much of what you pay for is sweetener, flavour and the natural carbohydrate and fat of concentrate. Run both numbers and you will rarely be fooled by packaging again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="false-economy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Cheap Is a False Economy: Amino Spiking
          </h2>

          <p>
            The standard way to measure protein in food does not measure protein directly. Methods such as Kjeldahl and Dumas measure nitrogen and multiply it by a conversion factor, usually 6.25, on the assumption that the nitrogen came from intact protein. That assumption is exactly what amino spiking exploits. Cheap nitrogen-containing ingredients such as glycine, taurine, creatine or other free amino acids raise the nitrogen reading, so a crude test reports more protein than the product really contains.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not a hypothetical. Starting in 2014, a wave of class-action lawsuits in the United States accused several sports nutrition brands of inflating protein claims this way, and trade publications covered the practice under the names protein spiking and nitrogen spiking. Many of those cases were allegations rather than findings, and the industry has tightened up considerably since, but the incentive has not gone away: whey costs far more per kilogram than glycine, and the price pressure is greatest at the bottom of the market.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spiked protein is a bad deal even when the free amino acids are harmless. Glycine and taurine are not the amino acids that drive muscle protein synthesis; leucine and the other essential amino acids are. A scoop labelled 25 g that contains 20 g of whey and 5 g of glycine gives you the muscle-building value of roughly 20 g of whey, which means your real cost per 25 g of useful protein is a quarter higher than the label suggests, and the cheap tub may no longer be cheap.
          </p>

          <p>
            The defence is mostly reading the ingredients list. Be wary when free amino acids, an amino acid matrix or a branded recovery blend sits inside or right after the protein blend, particularly if the product does not separate how much protein comes from whole protein. Prefer products with short ingredient lists, a single named protein source and an independent certification or published test results. Every pick in this guide passes that check on the label as it stood when we wrote it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label-tricks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Underdosed Blends, Big Scoops and Other Label Tricks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spiking is the dramatic version. The everyday version is subtler and perfectly legal: making a product look more generous than it is. The commonest trick is the oversized serving. A tub that advertises 30 g of protein per serving may define a serving as two heaped scoops weighing 45 g or more, which is how a protein share of 65 percent can still produce a big headline number. Always compare protein per gram of powder, not protein per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second trick is the proprietary blend. When a label lists a protein blend of whey concentrate, whey isolate, milk protein and soy protein with one combined weight, you cannot tell whether the expensive isolate is a meaningful share or a token pinch included for the front-of-tub claim. Ingredients are listed in descending order by weight, so the first one named is the largest, but that is all you learn. Cheap products lean on this because it lets them market an isolate while mostly selling a cheaper concentrate or plant protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is the underdosed serving. Some budget powders deliver 15 to 18 g of protein per scoop, which looks fine until you realise you need closer to two scoops to reach a dose that research supports for most adults after training. That doubles your real cost per serving. If you are unsure how much protein you need in a day or a meal, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">daily protein targets guide</a> walks through it by body weight and goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The last is the servings count on the front of the tub. It is usually honest arithmetic, but it is calculated from the brand's serving size, which may not be the serving you use. If you need 25 g of protein and the label serving gives 20 g, a tub advertised as 74 servings is really about 59 servings for you. Re-run the servings count with your own dose and you will see the true cost of each shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Why Whey Concentrate Is the Budget Sweet Spot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey protein concentrate is the least processed form of whey sold as a powder, and it is almost always the cheapest per gram of protein among dairy options. Most sports concentrates are sold as roughly 80 percent protein on a dry basis, with the rest made up mainly of lactose, milk fat and minerals. Isolate goes through extra filtration to reach 90 percent protein or more, and that extra step is a big part of why it costs more.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people the extra filtration buys very little. Both forms provide a complete amino acid profile with plenty of leucine, and research comparing them has not shown a meaningful difference in muscle gain when the protein dose is matched. The real difference is a few grams of lactose, carbohydrate and fat per scoop. If dairy sits well with you, concentrate gives you nearly the same result for less money, which is exactly what a budget pick should do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolate earns its premium in two situations. If you are lactose intolerant, the lower lactose content can be the difference between a shake you tolerate and one you do not, although many people with mild intolerance handle a single scoop of concentrate fine. And if you are counting every calorie on a strict cut, the leaner macros of isolate add up over months. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate comparison</a> goes through the trade-off in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why four of our six picks are concentrate or concentrate-led blends, and why our cheapest isolate is an unflavored one. Flavoring, sweeteners and cocoa add cost and dilute the protein share; an unflavored isolate skips them, which partly offsets the higher cost of the protein itself. If you want isolate on a budget, buying it plain and flavoring your own shakes is usually the way to get there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plant-budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cheap Plant Protein: What You Give Up and What to Check
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pea protein is the most affordable plant protein by a wide margin and the one most budget vegans and dairy-free buyers end up using. Single-ingredient pea isolates are often competitive with whey concentrate on cost per 25 g, and unflavored versions tend to be dense in protein by weight. The trade-offs are taste, texture and a slightly weaker amino acid profile.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea protein is lower in methionine than dairy and contains somewhat less leucine per gram than whey, so a matched dose is not quite equivalent. In practice the fix is simple: take a slightly larger serving, or use a blend that pairs pea with rice, which complements its weak spots. Research comparing pea and whey at decent doses alongside resistance training has generally found similar strength and muscle outcomes. Our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein rankings</a> cover the category in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger issue at the budget end is contaminants. Plant proteins draw minerals from soil, and that includes heavy metals. In its October 2025 testing of protein powders and shakes, Consumer Reports found that plant-based products on average contained substantially more lead than dairy-based ones. That does not mean every pea protein is unsafe, but it does mean that independent testing matters more for cheap plant protein than for almost anything else in this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the checklist for a budget plant powder is a little longer: a single named source or a disclosed blend, published test results or a recognised certification, and a brand willing to answer questions about heavy metals. If a plant protein you are considering cannot show any testing at all, the money you save is not worth the uncertainty. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">guide to protein powder safety</a> explains how to read heavy metal results and certification claims.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="save-more" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Ways to Cut Your Protein Cost Further
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy the biggest size you will finish before it goes stale. Five-pound tubs and bags almost always cost less per gram than two-pound ones, and unopened whey keeps for a long time when stored somewhere cool and dry. The exception is a flavor you have not tried: buy the small size first, because a cheap five-pound tub you cannot stand drinking is the most expensive protein you will ever own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Go unflavored where you can. Plain whey and pea protein disappear into oats, smoothies, yogurt and coffee, and you can flavor a shake with cocoa, cinnamon, instant coffee or a spoon of peanut butter for very little. Unflavored powders also tend to have a higher protein share per scoop because there is no cocoa, flavoring or sweetener taking up room. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein guide</a> lists plain options across price points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Time your purchases. Several of the brands here run frequent site-wide sales, and some are reliably discounted on retailer sale days. Subscribe-and-save discounts on retailer sites can also cut the cost of a product you buy every month anyway. Just recalculate cost per 25 g at the sale price instead of trusting the percentage-off badge, because a discount on an underdosed product can still lose to a full-price honest one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, remember that powder competes with food. Milk, Greek yogurt, cottage cheese, eggs, canned fish, dried lentils and chicken thighs are often competitive with protein powder on cost per 25 g of protein, depending on where you shop, and they bring other nutrients with them. Powder wins on convenience and calories per gram of protein, not always on price. If you only need a little extra protein each day, a carton of Greek yogurt may be the real budget pick.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pay-more" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Paying More Is Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you compete in a drug-tested sport, certification is not optional and it is worth paying for. A product carrying NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport has been screened for banned substances on a batch basis, and a failed test costs far more than a pricier tub. Some budget products do carry these certifications, but if the one you want does not, choose a certified product even if it costs more per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are lactose intolerant to the point that concentrate causes real symptoms, an isolate or a plant protein will be worth the extra money. Cheap protein you cannot digest comfortably is not cheap. The same logic applies to taste: the best-tasting powder you will drink every day beats a cheaper one that sits half-full in the cupboard, so a modest premium for a flavor you like is money well spent.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need more protein per calorie because you are dieting hard, the leaner macros of an isolate can justify a higher cost per gram, and our <a href="/best-high-protein-powder/">highest protein powder guide</a> ranks options on protein per scoop and per calorie. And if you want one brand that covers whey, isolate, casein and gainers in one place, our <a href="/best-optimum-nutrition-protein-powder/">Optimum Nutrition lineup guide</a> maps each tub to a goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the conclusion is reassuringly boring. A plain, honestly labelled whey concentrate or a well-tested pea protein bought in a large size gives you nearly everything an expensive powder does. Spend the difference on food, or on a gym membership you will use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A two-pound tub and a five-pound bag are not comparable at the shelf. Divide the price by the servings, then scale to 25 g of protein. It takes a minute on a phone calculator and it regularly reverses which product looks cheaper, especially when serving sizes differ.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the protein number without reading the ingredients</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Free amino acids such as glycine, taurine or creatine listed inside or beside the protein blend can inflate a nitrogen-based protein test. Prefer short ingredient lists with a single named protein source, and favor products with independent certification or published lab results.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the serving size behind the headline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big protein figure per serving often comes from a big serving. Check the serving weight in grams and divide protein by it. A product that needs two heaped scoops to hit its claim is not generous, it is simply asking you to use more powder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying five pounds of an untested flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The lowest cost per gram is only real if you finish the tub. Try a small size or a sample of an unfamiliar flavor first, or buy unflavored and season it yourself, before committing to the bulk size that makes budget protein cheap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping testing on cheap plant protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plant proteins tend to carry more heavy metals than dairy, and independent testing matters most where margins are thinnest. If a budget plant powder offers no certification or published results for lead and other metals, the small saving is not worth the uncertainty.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest way to buy protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a plain whey concentrate or a single-ingredient pea protein in the largest size you will finish, ideally on sale, and compare products on cost per 25 g of protein rather than tub price. Unflavored versions usually give the most protein per scoop for the money, and you can flavor them cheaply at home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is cheap protein powder bad for you?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not inherently. Many budget powders are simple whey or pea protein with a short ingredient list. The risks at the low end are inflated protein claims and, for plant products, heavy metals. Choose products with disclosed formulas and some form of independent testing, and a cheap powder can be just as good as an expensive one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate cost per gram of protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the price by the number of servings to get cost per serving, then divide that by the grams of protein per serving to get cost per gram. Multiply by 25 for cost per 25 g. Using the same unit for every product cancels out differences in tub size and scoop size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey concentrate as good as isolate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For building muscle, yes, when the protein dose is matched. Concentrate contains a little more lactose, fat and carbohydrate per scoop, which matters if you are lactose intolerant or counting calories very closely. For most people it delivers nearly the same result as isolate for noticeably less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is amino spiking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Amino spiking is adding cheap nitrogen-containing ingredients such as glycine, taurine or creatine to a protein product so that a nitrogen-based lab test reports more protein than is really there. It was the subject of a wave of US class-action lawsuits starting in 2014. Reading the ingredients list is the best defense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are budget plant protein powders safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many are, but plant proteins on average carry more heavy metals than dairy proteins, according to Consumer Reports testing in 2025. Look for published lab results or a recognized certification, and favor brands that answer questions about lead. Pregnant people and anyone with health conditions should ask a doctor or dietitian.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is protein powder cheaper than food?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, not always. Milk, eggs, Greek yogurt, canned fish, lentils and chicken thighs are often competitive per 25 g of protein, depending on prices where you live. Powder usually wins on convenience and on protein per calorie, so it is most useful when you are short on time or dieting.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

